Balaenidae consists of two genera: ''Eubalaena'' (right whales) and ''[[Balaena]]'' (the [[bowhead whale]], ''B. mysticetus''). Balaenidae was thought to have consisted of only one genus until studies done through the early 2000s reported that bowhead whales and right whales are [[morphology (biology)|morphologically]] (different skull shape) and [[phylogeny|phylogenically]] different. According to a study done by H. C. Rosenbaum (of the [[American Museum of Natural History]]) and colleagues, the North Pacific (''E. japonica'') and Southern right (''E. australis'') whales are more closely related to each other than to the North Atlantic right whale (''E. glacialis'').<ref name="Brownell">{{Cite journal |last1=Rosenbaum |first1=H. C. |last2=Brownell Jr. |first2=R. L. |last3=Schaeff |first3=M. W.B.C. |last4=Portway |first4=V. |last5=White |first5=B. N. |last6=Malik |first6=S. |last7=Pastene |first7=L. A. |last8=Patenaude |first8=N. J. |last9=Baker |first9=C. S. |last10=Goto |first10=M. |last11=Best |first11=P. |last12=Clapham |first12=P. J. |last13=Hamilton |first13=P. |last14=Moore |first14=M. |last15=Payne |first15=R. |date=2000 |title=World-wide genetic differentiation of ''Eubalaena'': Questioning the number of right whale species |journal=Molecular Ecology |volume=9 |issue=11 |pages=1793–1802 |bibcode=2000MolEc...9.1793R |doi=10.1046/j.1365-294x.2000.01066.x |pmid=11091315 |s2cid=7166876 |ref={{harvid|Rosenbaum and Brownell, Jr., et al.}} |first16=V. |last16=Rowntree |first17=C. T. |last17=Tynan |first18=J. L. |last18=Bannister |first19=R. |last19=Desalle}}</ref>

Cetotheriidae consists of only one living member: the pygmy right whale (''Caperea marginata''). The first descriptions date back to the 1840s of bones and [[baleen plate]]s resembling a smaller version of the right whale, and was named ''Balaena marginata''. In 1864, it was moved into the genus ''Caperea'' after a skull of another specimen was discovered. Six years later, the pygmy right whale was classified under the family Neobalaenidae.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Cousteau |first1=Jacques |title=Whales |last2=Paccalet |first2=Yves |publisher=H.N. Abrams |date=1986 |oclc=681455766}}</ref> Despite its name, the pygmy right whale is more [[genetics|genetically]] similar to rorquals and gray whales than to right whales.{{sfn|Bannister|2008|pp= 939–941}} A study published in 2012, based on bone structure, moved the pygmy right whale from the family Neobalaenidae to the family Cetotheriidae, making it a [[living fossil]]; Neobalaenidae was demoted to subfamily level as Neobalaeninae.<ref name="fordyce2012">{{Cite journal |last1=Fordyce |first1=R. E. |last2=Marx |first2=Felix G. |date=2012 |title=The pygmy right whale ''Caperea marginata'': the last of the cetotheres |journal=Proceedings of the Royal Society B |volume=280 |issue=1753 |article-number=20122645 |doi=10.1098/rspb.2012.2645 |pmc=3574355 |pmid=23256199}}{{open access}}</ref>

Rorquals consist of three genera (''[[Balaenoptera]],'' ''[[Megaptera]],'' and ''[[Eschrichtius]]'') and 11 species: the [[fin whale]] (''B. physalus''), the [[Sei whale]] (''B. borealis''), [[Bryde's whale]] (''B. brydei''), [[Eden's whale]] (''B. edeni''), [[Rice's whale]] (''B. ricei''), the [[blue whale]] (''B. musculus''), the [[common minke whale]] (''B. acutorostrata''), the [[Antarctic minke whale]] (''B. bonaerensis''), [[Omura's whale]] (''B. omurai''), the [[humpback whale]] (''M. novaeangliae''), and the [[gray whale]] (''E. robustus''). In a 2012 review of cetacean taxonomy, Alexandre Hassanin (of the [[Muséum National d'Histoire Naturelle]]) and colleagues suggested that, based on phylogenic criteria, there are four extant genera of rorquals. They recommend that the genus ''Balaenoptera'' be limited to the fin whale, have minke whales fall under the genus ''Pterobalaena'', and have ''Rorqualus'' contain the Sei whale, Bryde's whale, Eden's whale (and by extension Rice's whale), the blue whale, and Omura's whale.<ref name="Hassanin">{{Cite journal |last1=Hassanin |first1=Alexandre |last2=Delsucc |first2=Frédéric |last3=Ropiquet |first3=Anne |last4=Hammere |first4=Catrin |last5=Van Vuurenf |first5=Bettine J. |last6=Mattheef |first6=Conrad |last7=Ruiz-Garcia |first7=Manuel |last8=Catzeflisc |first8=François |last9=Areskough |first9=Veronika |last10=Thanh Nguyena |first10=Trung |last11=Coulouxj |first11=Arnaud |date=2012 |title=Histoire évolutive des Cetartiodactyla (Mammalia, Laurasiatheria) racontée par l'analyse des génomes mitochondriaux |journal=Comptes Rendus Biologies |language=fr |volume=335 |issue=1 |pages=32–50 |doi=10.1016/j.crvi.2011.11.002 |pmid=22226162 |ref={{harvid|Hassanin and Ropiquet, et al.}}|doi-access=free }}</ref> The gray whale was formerly classified in its own family. The two populations, one in the [[Sea of Okhotsk]] and [[Sea of Japan]] and the other in [[Pacific Ocean|eastern Pacific]] are thought to be genetically and physiologically dissimilar.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Nakamura |first1=G. |last2=Kato |first2=H. |date=2014 |script-title=ja:日本沿岸域に近年(1990–2005 年)出現したコククジラEschrichtius robustus の骨学的特徴, 特に頭骨形状から見た北太平洋西部系群と東部系群交流の可能性 |trans-title=Possibility of the North Pacific Western group and eastern group exchanges as seen from the osteologic features of the gray whale ''Eschrichtius robustus'' recently in the coastal area of Japan (1990–2005), especially from the skull shape |url=https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/mammalianscience/54/1/54_73/_pdf |format=PDF |journal=哺乳類科学 |language=ja |volume=54 |issue=1 |pages=73–88 |doi=10.11238/mammalianscience.54.73}}{{open access}}</ref> However, there is some discussion as to whether the gray whale should be classified into its own family, or as a rorqual, with recent studies favoring the latter.<ref name=":1">{{Cite journal |last=Marx |first=Felix |date=2010 |title=The More the Merrier? A Large Cladistic Analysis of Mysticetes, and Comments on the Transition from Teeth to Baleen |journal=Journal of Mammalian Evolution |volume=18 |issue=2 |pages=77–100 |doi=10.1007/s10914-010-9148-4 |s2cid=24684836}}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite journal |last1=Nikaido |first1=Masato |last2=Hamilton |first2=Healy |last3=Makino |first3=Hitomi |date=2006 |title=Baleen Whale Phylogeny and a Past Extensive Radiation Event Revealed by SINE Insertion Analysis |journal=Molecular Biology and Evolution |volume=23 |issue=5 |pages=866–73 |doi=10.1093/molbev/msj071 |pmid=16330660 |doi-access=free}}</ref><ref name="Sasaki">{{Cite journal |last1=Sasaki |first1=Takeshi |last2=Nikaido |first2=Masato |last3=Hamilton |first3=Healy |last4=Goto |first4=Mutsuo |last5=Kato |first5=Hidehiro |last6=Kanda |first6=Naohisa |last7=Pastene |first7=Luis |last8=Cao |first8=Ying |last9=Fordyce |first9=R. |last10=Hasegawa |first10=Masami |last11=Okada |first11=Norihiro |date=2005 |title=Mitochondrial Phylogenetics and Evolution of Mysticete Whales |journal=Systematic Biology |volume=54 |issue=1 |pages=77–90 |doi=10.1080/10635150590905939 |pmid=15805012 |ref={{harvid|Sasaki and Nikaido, et al.}} |doi-access=free}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{Cite journal |last1=Áranson |first1=Úlfur |last2=Lammers |first2=Fritjof |last3=Kumar |first3=Vikas |last4=Nilsson |first4=Maria |date=2018 |title=Whole-genome sequencing of the blue whale and other rorquals finds signatures for introgressive gene flow |journal=Science Advances |volume=4 |issue=4 |page=10 |bibcode=2018SciA....4.9873A |doi=10.1126/sciadv.aap9873 |pmc=5884691 |pmid=29632892}}</ref>

===Etymology===
The taxonomic name "Mysticeti" ({{IPAc-en|ˌ|m|ɪ|s|t|ɪ|ˈ|s|iː|t|aɪ}}) apparently derives from a translation error in early copies of [[Aristotle]]'s ''[[History of Animals|Historia Animalium]]'' (in [[Ancient Greek]]), in which "{{lang|grc|ὁ μῦς τὸ κῆτος}}" (''ho mus to kētos'', "the mouse, the whale so called") was mistakenly translated as "{{lang|grc|ὁ μυστικῆτος}}" (''ho mustikētos'', "the Mysticetus"), which D. W. Rice (of the [[Society for Marine Mammalogy]]) in {{Harvnb|Rice|1998}} assumed was an ironic reference to the animals' great size.{{sfn|Bannister|2008|pp=[https://books.google.com/books?id=2rkHQpToi9sC&pg=PA80 80–81]}} An alternate name for the parvorder is "Mystacoceti" (from Greek {{lang|grc|μύσταξ}} "mustache" + {{lang|grc|κῆτος}} "whale"), which, although obviously more appropriate and occasionally used in the past, has been superseded by "Mysticeti" ([[junior synonym]]).{{sfn|Bannister|2008|pp=[https://books.google.com/books?id=2rkHQpToi9sC&pg=PA80 80–81]}}

Mysticetes are also known as baleen whales for their [[baleen]], which they use to sieve [[plankton]] and other small organisms from the water. The term "baleen" ([[Middle English]] ''baleyn, ballayne, ballien, bellane'', etc.) is an archaic word for "whale", which came from [[Old French]] ''[[wikt:baleine#Old French|baleine]]'', derived from the [[Latin]] word ''[[wikt:balaena|balæna]]'', derived itself from the [[Ancient Greek]] ''[[wikt:φάλλαινα|φάλλαινα]] (phállaina)''.<ref name="SOED">{{Cite book |title=Shorter Oxford English dictionary |publisher=Oxford University Press |date=2007 |isbn=978-0-19-920687-2 |page=3804}}</ref>

Right whales got their name because of [[whaling|whalers]] preferring them over other species; they were essentially the "right whale" to catch.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Dolin |first=Eric Jay |url={{Google books|plainurl=yes|id=KlVe8hYUpU4C|page=22}} |title=Leviathan: The History of Whaling in America |publisher=W.W. Norton & Co. |date=2007 |isbn=978-0-393-06057-7 |page=22}}{{open access}}</ref>

===Differences between families===
[[File:Baleen whale sizes.JPG|thumb|Baleen whales vary considerably in size and shape, depending on their feeding behavior.]]
Rorquals use throat pleats to expand their mouths, which allow them to feed more effectively. However, rorquals need to build up water pressure in order to expand their mouths, leading to a lunge-feeding behavior. Lunge-feeding is where a whale rams a [[bait ball]] (a swarm of [[bait fish|small fish]]) at high speed. Rorquals generally have streamlined physiques to reduce drag in the water while doing this.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Potvin |first1=J. |last2=Goldbogen |first2=J. A. |last3=Shadwick |first3=R. E. |date=2009 |title=Passive versus active engulfment: verdict from trajectory simulations of lunge-feeding fin whales ''Balaenoptera physalus'' |journal=Journal of the Royal Society Interface |volume=6 |issue=40 |pages=1005–1025 |doi=10.1098/rsif.2008.0492 |pmc=2827442 |pmid=19158011 |bibcode=2009JRSI....6.1005P }}{{open access}}</ref>
Balaenids rely on their huge heads, as opposed to the rorquals' throat pleats, to feed effectively. This feeding behavior allows them to grow very big and bulky, without the necessity for a streamlined body. They have [[callosities]], unlike other whales, with the exception of the bowhead whale.{{sfn|Bannister|2008|loc=[https://books.google.com/books?id=2rkHQpToi9sC&pg=PA80 p. 80]}} Rorquals have a higher proportion of muscle tissue and tend to be negatively buoyant, whereas right whales have a higher proportion of blubber and are positively buoyant.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Lockyer |first=C. |date=1976 |title=Body weights of some species of large whales |journal=Journal du Conseil International pour l'Exploration de la Mer |volume=36 |issue=3 |pages=259–273 |doi=10.1093/icesjms/36.3.259}}</ref> Gray whales are easily distinguished from the other rorquals by their sleet-gray color, dorsal ridges (knuckles on the back), and their gray-white scars left from parasites. As with the other rorquals, their throat pleats increase the capacity of their throats, allowing them to filter larger volumes of water at once. Gray whales are bottom-feeders, meaning they sift through sand to get their food. They usually turn on their sides, scoop up sediment into their mouths and filter out [[benthic]] creatures like [[amphipod]]s, which leave noticeable marks on their heads.<ref>{{Cite book |last1=Jones |first1=Mary Lou |title=The Gray Whale: Eschrichtius robustus |last2=Swartz |first2=Steven L. |last3=Leatherwood |first3=Stephen |publisher=Elsevier Science |date=1984 |isbn=978-0-12-389180-8 |editor-last=Jones |editor-first=Mary Lou |pages=33–34, 423–424 |chapter=A Review of Gray Whale Feeding Ecology |editor-last2=L. Swartz |editor-first2=Steven |editor-last3=Leatherwood |editor-first3=Stephen |chapter-url={{Google books|plainurl=yes|id=GfGITi5NmJoC|page=423}}}}{{open access}}</ref> The pygmy right whale is easily confused with minke whales because of their similar characteristics, such as their small size, dark gray tops, light gray bottoms, and light eye patches.{{sfn|Bannister|2008|loc=[https://books.google.com/books?id=2rkHQpToi9sC&pg=PA80 p. 80]}}

